- Retirement contributions: each 401(k) or Roth 401(k) row says when the tax on it is paid ("Tax on this money: paid later, the usual. Change ›"), and the tap offers Pay the tax later, Pay the tax now or Some of each; some of each keeps what goes in now ("Now $400 a month in all"), saves two rows with the employer's match kept on the pay-the-tax-later one (a $0 "Roth 401(k) at work" is added on Net worth when there is none), says what paying it now costs ("About $190 a year more in federal income tax ...: about $21 a month less take-home pay"), gives the rule of thumb and, for people 50 and over paid over $150,000, the catch-up rule; after saving, one sentence, and "When the tax is paid" under the rows holds the tax and the mix of your money when retirement starts; Paycheck shows the Roth 401(k) line from these rows and Work benefits counts both rows toward the full match.
